WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.830
-: So I just spent all that time demonstrating

00:01.830 --> 00:04.500
working on feature branches, but so far,

00:04.500 --> 00:09.060
none of the work that Colt/me did or Stevie did.

00:09.060 --> 00:11.130
None of that work is integrated

00:11.130 --> 00:13.893
into the main branch or the master branch.

00:13.893 --> 00:17.310
So that obviously is an important part of working

00:17.310 --> 00:20.760
on feature branches, is integrating your changes in.

00:20.760 --> 00:23.730
And we have a couple of options for how we do this.

00:23.730 --> 00:25.710
One would just be merge at will,

00:25.710 --> 00:28.740
whenever anybody wants to merge their changes in,

00:28.740 --> 00:30.270
just go for it.

00:30.270 --> 00:32.130
Just merge them into main.

00:32.130 --> 00:34.350
Obviously that could be fraught with problems,

00:34.350 --> 00:36.750
especially on larger teams with lots and lots

00:36.750 --> 00:39.300
of collaborators, lots of new features.

00:39.300 --> 00:42.030
If there's no testing going on beforehand,

00:42.030 --> 00:44.010
if there's no conversation or discussion

00:44.010 --> 00:46.890
going on beforehand, that could get way out of control.

00:46.890 --> 00:50.580
And you would never do that on a real product with users

00:50.580 --> 00:52.710
and you know, things at stake.

00:52.710 --> 00:55.920
Maybe on a fun hobby project with friends, I guess.

00:55.920 --> 00:58.245
So another option that's similar is,

00:58.245 --> 01:00.990
you merge your own branches in,

01:00.990 --> 01:03.270
but only after you have discussed it.

01:03.270 --> 01:06.270
You've sent an email or a chat message

01:06.270 --> 01:08.970
or some sort of, had a conversation

01:08.970 --> 01:11.280
with your team in some way about

01:11.280 --> 01:13.950
whether you should merge those changes in or not.

01:13.950 --> 01:15.900
And then there is the third option,

01:15.900 --> 01:18.630
which is what we're going to talk about in the next video,

01:18.630 --> 01:20.430
something called Pull Requests.

01:20.430 --> 01:21.960
But first, I just want to do another

01:21.960 --> 01:23.580
demonstration of merging.

01:23.580 --> 01:26.070
So if you wanna just get to Pull Requests, skip this video.

01:26.070 --> 01:27.840
But I'm just gonna quickly demonstrate.

01:27.840 --> 01:31.110
On Colt's computer,

01:31.110 --> 01:33.420
we have this pricing table branch, right?

01:33.420 --> 01:36.540
This is what the pricing table branch looks like.

01:36.540 --> 01:40.140
But the main branch currently doesn't have any of this.

01:40.140 --> 01:42.930
So let's say I've talked it over with Stevie.

01:42.930 --> 01:44.490
We're just a two person team,

01:44.490 --> 01:46.200
well one person, one chicken team.

01:46.200 --> 01:50.430
And I say, okay, I'm ready to merge this in, I think.

01:50.430 --> 01:52.620
Let's say Stevie pulls it down,

01:52.620 --> 01:54.600
Stevie takes a look, we consent, all right

01:54.600 --> 01:55.500
let's merge it in.

01:55.500 --> 01:56.730
Somebody merges it in.

01:56.730 --> 02:00.150
Let's say it's me, I'm the owner of the project.

02:00.150 --> 02:03.090
So I'm currently on the pricing table branch.

02:03.090 --> 02:04.800
I'm gonna switch to Master.

02:04.800 --> 02:08.400
Git switch to main--old habits.

02:08.400 --> 02:10.156
And then it's always good to just pull down

02:10.156 --> 02:11.943
to see if you've missed anything.

02:13.140 --> 02:15.750
Do we have any new work from main?

02:15.750 --> 02:16.950
No, we don't.

02:16.950 --> 02:20.073
Okay, so now I'm just gonna merge.

02:21.090 --> 02:23.220
And what is the branch I'm trying to merge in?

02:23.220 --> 02:28.220
Pricing table and it looks like, well easy, fast forward.

02:28.440 --> 02:30.360
So nothing bad, no commits,

02:30.360 --> 02:33.390
or sorry, no conflicts, no merge commit needed.

02:33.390 --> 02:34.410
Fast forward merge.

02:34.410 --> 02:37.320
And we see the pricing table shows up here.

02:37.320 --> 02:40.560
Great, I'll refresh my view here.

02:40.560 --> 02:42.720
I'm now on the main branch.

02:42.720 --> 02:44.820
We see the same thing as before.

02:44.820 --> 02:47.820
Okay, so we just integrated that feature branch

02:47.820 --> 02:52.820
and now I can push main back up to origin main.

02:52.890 --> 02:54.450
So I'm gonna do exactly that.

02:54.450 --> 02:58.203
Git push origin main,

03:00.930 --> 03:02.330
and then we'll go to GitHub.

03:04.380 --> 03:07.920
And now main includes the code from,

03:07.920 --> 03:09.690
no I'm on the wrong branch.

03:09.690 --> 03:12.150
Main includes the code from pricing table.

03:12.150 --> 03:14.850
We see whatever work I had done.

03:14.850 --> 03:16.440
It's now on this branch.

03:16.440 --> 03:18.120
So nothing new. We just merged.

03:18.120 --> 03:21.660
And then on Stevie's side of things, wherever he lives,

03:21.660 --> 03:24.060
the next step for him would be to make sure to

03:24.060 --> 03:26.190
get that new version of the main branch.

03:26.190 --> 03:28.020
Whenever he goes back to main or whenever

03:28.020 --> 03:30.810
he needs that new work, go back to main branch.

03:30.810 --> 03:33.060
So git switch main,

03:33.060 --> 03:35.640
he could fetch or he could just pull,

03:35.640 --> 03:37.833
git pull origin main.

03:39.330 --> 03:43.560
And now Stevie has this new work on his main branch.

03:43.560 --> 03:46.500
He has the whole pricing table and all that stuff.

03:46.500 --> 03:49.560
And then he could merge in his nav bar branch

03:49.560 --> 03:52.140
if they both agree it's appropriate,

03:52.140 --> 03:54.630
he could merge in nav bar into main

03:54.630 --> 03:55.710
and then push that up.

03:55.710 --> 03:58.080
And then Colt would then go pull that down

03:58.080 --> 03:59.970
and we'd repeat over and over.

03:59.970 --> 04:01.110
So that's just a quick version

04:01.110 --> 04:03.381
of merging a feature branch in.

04:03.381 --> 04:06.150
It would be best to delete that branch locally

04:06.150 --> 04:08.940
if we agree that it's, you know, we're good to go.

04:08.940 --> 04:10.320
We merged in pricing table,

04:10.320 --> 04:11.850
we don't need that branch anymore.

04:11.850 --> 04:13.980
Delete it, delete it off of GitHub.

04:13.980 --> 04:16.620
But just to keep things short and easy for you to follow.

04:16.620 --> 04:18.810
If you do take a look at this repository,

04:18.810 --> 04:21.780
I'm not going to delete that branch this way.

04:21.780 --> 04:23.370
You'll just have that history.

04:23.370 --> 04:24.870
And in the next video,

04:24.870 --> 04:27.660
we're gonna talk about the more common way

04:27.660 --> 04:29.700
of merging feature branches in

04:29.700 --> 04:33.933
or requesting to merge them in, which is a Pull Request.
